{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1974 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7df58fa006ca16264ab106321f2fa5f6c28cd0b6198f6af744b7badcd20f9de9" :edb/db-before-t 1973 :edb/db-after-t 1974 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535286 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:14:38.913Z" 13194139535286 true] [17592186161798 1100 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a$.\n\n|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac a2$ | $a$ |\n| $X=1$ | $a$ | $\\dfrac 52a$ |" 13194139535286 false] [17592186161798 1100 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac a2$ | $a$ |\n| $X=1$ | $a$ | $\\dfrac 52a$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186161798 1145 "The support of $X$ is\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0,1\\}\n$$\n\nthe support of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{0,1\\}\n$$\n\nand therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS= S_X\\times S_Y = \\left\\{(0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0, 0) + f(0, 1) + f(1, 0) + f(1, 1) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{a}{2} + a + a + \\frac{5}{2}a &= 1 \\\\\n5a &= 1 \\\\\na &= \\frac{1}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 false] [17592186161798 1145 "The support of $X$ is $S_X = \\{0,1\\},$ the support of $Y$ is $S_Y = \\{0,1\\},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS= S_X\\times S_Y = \\left\\{(0,0),  (0,1),  (1,0),  (1,1) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{a}{2}+a+a+\\frac{5}{2}a & =1 \\\\ 5a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186161808 1100 "Compute $P\\Big((X,Y) \\in \\{(2,3), (6,1) \\}\\Big)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |" 13194139535286 false] [17592186161808 1100 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P\\Big( (X,Y) \\in \\{(2,3), (6,1) \\}\\Big)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 true] [17592186161808 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP((X, Y) ∈ {(2, 3), (6, 1)}) &= P((X, Y) = (2, 3)) + P((X, Y) = (6, 1)) \\\\\n&= f(2, 3) + f(6, 1) \\\\\n&= 0.25 + 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0.55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 false] [17592186161808 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in {(2,3),(6,1)}) & =P((X,Y)=(2,3))+P((X,Y)=(6,1)) \\\\  & =f(2,3)+f(6,1) \\\\  & =0.25+0.3 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186161809 1100 "Compute $P\\left(X\\cdot Y \\geq 4 \\right)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |" 13194139535286 false] [17592186161809 1100 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left( X\\cdot Y \\geq 4 \\right)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 true] [17592186161809 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\cdot Y \\ge 4) &= P((X, Y) ∈ {(1, 4), (2, 2), (2, 4)}) \\\\\n&= P((X, Y) = (1, 4)) + P((X, Y) = (2, 2)) + P((X, Y) = (2, 4)) \\\\\n&= f(1, 4) + f(2, 2) + f(2, 4) \\\\\n&= 0.05 + 0.25 + 0.15 \\\\\n&= 0.45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 false] [17592186161809 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\cdot Y\\ge 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(1,4),(2,2),(2,4)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(1,4))+P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(1,4)+f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.25+0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186161998 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341749 13194139535286 true] [17592186161998 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341756 13194139535286 true] [17592186161998 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341763 13194139535286 true] [17592186161998 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341770 13194139535286 true] [17592186162008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341700 13194139535286 true] [17592186162008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341707 13194139535286 true] [17592186162008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341714 13194139535286 true] [17592186162008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341721 13194139535286 true] [17592186162009 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341728 13194139535286 true] [17592186162009 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341735 13194139535286 true] [17592186162009 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341742 13194139535286 true] [17592186162212 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535286 true] [17592186162212 1100 "| $f$ | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions are true?\n\n1. $0 \\le f(x, y) \\le 1$ for all $(x, y)$ in $S$\n2. $∑_((x, y) ∈ S)f(x, y) = 1$\n3. $f(x, y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function." 13194139535286 false] [17592186162212 1100 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions are true?\n\n- $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$\n- ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$\n- $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function." 13194139535286 true] [17592186162212 1145 "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition I is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition II is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.1+0.25+0.25+0.05+0.15+0.2=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions I and II, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535286 true] [17592186162214 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535286 true] [17592186162214 1100 "|  | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X = 2andY \\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 false] [17592186162214 1100 "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 true] [17592186162214 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,2),(2,4)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186162215 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535286 true] [17592186162215 1100 "|  | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X < 2orY = 6)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 false] [17592186162215 1100 "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X<2\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y=6)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 true] [17592186162215 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y=6) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(1,2),(1,4),(1,6),(2,6)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(1,4)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,6))+P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(1,4)+f(1,6)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186162224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535286 true] [17592186162224 1100 "|  | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X = 0, Y = 1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 false] [17592186162224 1100 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X=0,Y=1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 true] [17592186162224 1145 "Using the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341700 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341701 13194139535286 true] [17592186341700 1089 "q-109687" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341700 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535286 true] [17592186341700 1100 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.12$ | $0.23$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X+Y=1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535286 true] [17592186341700 1113 #uuid "dfa1fd5a-68af-54d5-af73-981485a1c099" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341700 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y=1) & =P(X=0,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=0) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341702 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341703 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341704 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341705 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341706 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1041 "selection" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341705 13194139535286 true] [17592186341701 1110 #uuid "59208cd2-af20-5032-87ee-a211c6848542" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341702 1034 "0.24" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535286 true] [17592186341702 1111 #uuid "9b4aab37-736c-5fb1-a20e-5a77864e537e" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341703 1034 "0.54" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535286 true] [17592186341703 1111 #uuid "46f7fb0e-8062-5352-a824-12a4d8a2fb07" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341704 1034 "0.35" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341704 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535286 true] [17592186341704 1111 #uuid "765b86a9-70ef-5de6-8f8c-b0ecc5b70364" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341705 1034 "0.45" 13194139535286 true] [17592186341705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535286 true] [17592186341705 1111 #uuid "f8df8694-9593-5ce7-8e7f-35bad4bb94a7" 13194139535286 true] [1759218634